    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "The wider family and marital status",
     "texto": [
      "The masculine plural covers both genders: **los abuelos** are grandfather and grandmother, **los tíos** uncle and aunt, and **los hijos** sons and daughters.\n\nIn Spanish we use **de** to indicate family relationships: *el padre de mi madre es mi abuelo*.\n\nTo talk about marital status we usually use the verb **estar** with adjectives agreeing in gender: **estoy casado / casada**, **está soltero / soltera**.\n\nTo ask questions we say: *¿Estás casado?*, *¿Tienes hijos?*. And reply: *Tengo dos hijos: un hijo y una hija*.\n\nFor relative age we use **mayor** (older) and **menor** (younger): *mi hermano mayor*."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "gramatica",
     "titulo": "Marital status with estar",
     "texto": [
      "The marital status adjective agrees with the person we are speaking about:\n\n- **Masculine**: Pablo está casado.\n- **Feminine**: Lucía está casada.\n\nUse *soltero / soltera* for people who are not married."
     ],
     "ejemplos": [
